// mlir-gen's "unsupported cast" IS A SELF-DIAGNOSED MALFUNCTION AND MUST FAIL
// THE COMPILE.
//
// MEASURED at the parent commit (0fb56500), verbatim, on exactly this program:
//   logosc rc = 0
//   mlir_gen: unsupported cast
//   logosc: wrote <obj>            ← 1288 bytes ON DISK
// The assignment `a = s as f64;` lowered to NOTHING, `a` kept its previous
// value, and the compiler reported success. A compiler that prints an error and
// returns 0 is a gate that lies, and every `pass` test downstream of one is
// certifying a program it never compiled.
//
// ⚠ THE OLD BEHAVIOUR WAS NOT EVEN CONSISTENTLY WRONG, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVED.
// `coerce_to` returned a bare nullptr, so whether the compile failed depended on
// WHO CONSUMED IT: measured at the same commit,
//   fn f(s: str) -> f64 { return s as f64; }   → rc 1, no object
// because the `return` statement has its own R2 report, while the assignment
// above → rc 0, object written. Same malfunction, opposite verdicts, decided by
// the syntactic context. The fix reports at the SITE, so the consumer no longer
// decides.
//
// ⚠ THE ASSERTION HERE IS THE MESSAGE; THE EXIT CODE IS ASSERTED SEPARATELY, and
// it has to be. `run_test.sh`'s fail mode runs logosc under `|| true` and greps
// only stderr — it CANNOT distinguish rc 0 from rc 1, so a fail test alone would
// have passed at the parent commit too, i.e. it would be decoration. The rc and
// the absent object file are pinned by `logos_00_mlir_gen_exit_code`
// (mlir_gen_exit_code_gate.sh), which compiles THIS FILE and asserts both.
//
// ⚠ WHY A `str as f64` AND NOT THE `avg(str)` THAT FOUND THIS. The wql front end
// now refuses `avg` over a non-numeric column (`wql_agg_avg_str_arg_fail`), so
// that program never reaches mlir-gen again — a fixture routed through it would
// stop measuring the backend the moment the front end was fixed. This program
// depends on nothing but the cast.
//
// ⚠ IT IS ALSO NOT THE ROOT. `str as f64` should not survive SEMA at all; that
// is the open cast blocklist→allowlist arc (sema_expr.cpp, where the
// float↔pointer instance of the same class was already closed one type at a
// time). When sema learns to refuse it, THIS expectation changes to sema's
// diagnostic and the gate needs a different unsupported cast — which is the
// correct outcome, not a regression.
package test;

fn main() -> i32 {
    let s: str = "x";
    let mut a: f64 = 1.0f64;
    a = s as f64;
    if a > 0.0f64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
